WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.960
- I'm hanging with Marlon Wayans

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.170
and a whole audience of Gen Zers,

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.780
give it up.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.468
With her powerful words of wisdom,

00:00:07.468 --> 00:00:09.600
my next guest has been a guiding light

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.450
to her followers who include
a whole slew of celebrities,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.700
including Nicole Richie
and Kerry Washington.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.510
But her mantras aren't
just limited to the stars.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.230
Countless people turn to her insights

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.339
to keep themselves centered and sane.

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:24.097
Her new book is called,
"Heart Talk: The Journal:

00:00:24.097 --> 00:00:27.670
"52 Weeks of Self-Love,
Self-Care, and Self-Discovery".

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:28.920
And it's out right now.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.064
Please everybody welcome Cleo Wade!

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:36.064
(upbeat music)
(audience applauds)

00:00:41.114 --> 00:00:41.947
COVID hug!

00:00:45.192 --> 00:00:47.230
The entrance is so weird now

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.380
'cause no one can touch each other,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.720
but we've made it to
being in the same room,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.220
so this is progress in my opinion.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.320
- Yeah, everyone needs like a secret thing

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.720
that's like a signal for a hug.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:56.850
- Yeah, exactly.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.480
I liked yours actually,
it was really nice.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.170
I felt warm and fuzzy.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.383
Well, say hello to Marlon.

00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:02.216
- Hi.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.940
- All right, well Cleo,
for those who don't know,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.496
give a little background
on how you became a poet

00:01:08.496 --> 00:01:10.650
and author and source of inspiration,

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.130
like how did this all happen for you?

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.240
- You know, I went to poetry summer camp

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.273
as a little girl.

00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:20.161
And growing up in the south,

00:01:20.161 --> 00:01:21.610
as most of the time,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:23.730
the only young Black girl in the room

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.030
with two divorced parents,

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.920
I kind of wrote my whole childhood

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.430
whether it was in my camps

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.058
or in my own journals and diaries.

00:01:31.058 --> 00:01:33.590
And I wasn't really brave enough

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:34.480
to put it on the internet.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.890
I kind of shared it with
my small friend group

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:37.960
and I was like, I think I'm gonna start

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.970
to put it on the internet, I don't know.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.403
And I did, and it just kind of took off.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.280
- Well, your new book is a journal

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.450
that expands on your book "Heart Talk",

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.689
so why did you want to write this one?

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:52.820
The journal thing is
cool that goes with it.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.410
- Yeah, you know, when
I went on my book tour

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.838
for my second book, "Where to Begin",

00:01:56.838 --> 00:01:58.749
"Heart Talk" had been in the world

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:00.300
for about a year and a half.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.970
And so everyone was
bringing me their copies

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.860
of "Heart Talk" 'cause
everyone was getting

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.070
the new copy of the new
book that day, right?

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.490
So they had their old
ones they were bringing,

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.510
and when I tell you,

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.040
all of these amazing people
had like drawn on it,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.260
ripped it in, written in the margins,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.870
I mean, everyone's book
had notes everywhere,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.802
because I leave a lot of
room and space and place

00:02:18.802 --> 00:02:21.571
for people to kind of collaborate with me.

00:02:21.571 --> 00:02:23.370
Like, that's one of my favorite things

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.700
I get to see from my readers.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.210
And I just went through "Heart Talk"

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.597
and I took out 52 things,

00:02:28.597 --> 00:02:30.660
and I was like, we could
really work on these things.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.650
What does it look like to
really take a whole week

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.080
to just work on boundaries?

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.786
What's the mantra for boundaries?

00:02:36.786 --> 00:02:37.619
(Kelly groans)

00:02:37.619 --> 00:02:38.720
What does it look like
to really take a week

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:39.860
and work on shame?

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.080
What's the mantra for shame
that you'll go back to?

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.143
What are the questions
that help me with shame?

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.510
I mean, even in the
first page of the book,

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:50.343
it literally just says,

00:02:50.343 --> 00:02:52.497
"How are you actually doing?"

00:02:52.497 --> 00:02:54.885
You know, because when do we answer that?

00:02:54.885 --> 00:02:57.400
- And sometimes it's a
hard question to answer,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.486
'cause we stay so busy.

00:02:58.486 --> 00:03:00.300
And we stay so busy

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.210
so we don't have to answer
the question sometimes.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.750
- We're busy and for
some reason, how are you

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:05.735
turns us into a robot.

00:03:05.735 --> 00:03:07.960
Like, we're always like, good, fine!

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:08.793
No, amazing!

00:03:08.793 --> 00:03:10.006
I'm like, it's a pandemic,

00:03:10.006 --> 00:03:11.570
you're not fine.
(audience laughs)

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:12.750
- You're not amazing.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.436
If you're doing amazing than you suck.

00:03:14.436 --> 00:03:16.830
'Cause everyone else isn't.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.005
- And so, I'm like, listen,

00:03:18.005 --> 00:03:20.246
if that's how you think you are,

00:03:20.246 --> 00:03:22.008
then you didn't hear the question.

00:03:22.008 --> 00:03:23.060
- Yeah.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.220
- Because our lives--

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.260
- [Kelly] I think people might be afraid

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:26.093
of the question.

00:03:26.093 --> 00:03:26.926
- Yeah.

00:03:26.926 --> 00:03:28.240
- All right, so Marlon,

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.490
what's a mantra you kind of live by?

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.500
- Don't think, do.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.333
- Oh, that's a good one.

00:03:33.333 --> 00:03:34.690
- Yeah, everybody talks about

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:36.300
giving themself so much anxiety

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.475
thinking about how to be successful,

00:03:38.475 --> 00:03:40.820
and I don't think about it, you just do,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.170
and success will happen.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.380
Just make sure you love
and just do, it happens.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.570
- Yeah, I love that.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.750
I'm gonna stitch that on a pillow.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.920
Well, we have a whole
audience of young people

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.376
trying to navigate the ups
and downs of their lives,

00:03:53.376 --> 00:03:55.732
and I know they have
some questions for Cleo,

00:03:55.732 --> 00:03:58.470
so Marlon, will you answer too?

00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.394
Do you mind?

00:03:59.394 --> 00:04:00.227
- Go for it.

00:04:00.227 --> 00:04:01.210
- You seem to be awesome at it.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.560
Just saying, you should write a book.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:03.860
All right, so first we have Bailey

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.409
who's in McRae, Arkansas.

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:07.363
What's going on with you, Bailey?

00:04:07.363 --> 00:04:09.370
- Hey, Kelly, hey, Cleo.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:10.341
I'm 23 years old

00:04:10.341 --> 00:04:12.965
and I'm still not sure
what my passion is in life,

00:04:12.965 --> 00:04:16.410
and I went to school and
I graduated last year

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:17.586
with my degree in psychology,

00:04:17.586 --> 00:04:20.581
and since then I've been
working in the bank industry,

00:04:20.581 --> 00:04:23.140
because that's the
first job that opened up

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.131
as soon I graduated,

00:04:24.131 --> 00:04:27.203
but I've always felt led to help people

00:04:27.203 --> 00:04:29.009
and walk with them in the darkest moments

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:31.300
and shine like a light on them,

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:32.515
and just be there for them,

00:04:32.515 --> 00:04:34.290
so with that, I feel like

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.035
I should pursue my degree in counseling,

00:04:36.035 --> 00:04:39.130
but I'm not sure if that's just a dream

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.404
that's associated with my past,

00:04:40.404 --> 00:04:41.847
because it was complicated,

00:04:41.847 --> 00:04:45.100
or if that's something that
I really need to pursue

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.490
as a passion.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.610
- Cleo, you wanna go first?

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:52.274
- You know, I think
that if you keep hearing

00:04:52.274 --> 00:04:55.050
a little voice whispering in your head

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:56.210
that this is what I want

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.690
and this is what I wanna do,

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.980
then you should continue listening to it

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.270
as the voice gets louder and louder.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.874
I don't know a single person

00:05:04.874 --> 00:05:07.310
who has felt that every job they ever had

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.780
or every day when they went to work,

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.940
it was the best day of their life

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.320
or their ideal job,

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.540
but I will say that almost like in dating,

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.040
it's like you kind of
know you met the right one

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.641
because you definitely know
what the wrong one looks like.

00:05:19.641 --> 00:05:23.260
So don't be afraid to just
stay open to the lesson

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.796
even if it's not your ideal situation.

00:05:26.796 --> 00:05:28.700
- What do you think, Marlon?

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.800
- Girl, get that money.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.030
You can think about helping people later.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:34.892
Get that money now, collect it.

00:05:34.892 --> 00:05:36.390
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:39.310
And once you get rich, you
can be a philanthropist,

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.210
you can open up your
own counseling session,

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:42.830
you can do what you want,

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.981
get that money, but make
sure you love your job.

00:05:45.981 --> 00:05:47.790
If you love it, stay,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.570
and if you don't, go.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.350
But girl, it's better to go

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.565
after you've filled your pocket with loot.

00:05:53.565 --> 00:05:56.944
(Kelly and Cleo laughing)

00:05:56.944 --> 00:06:00.238
- I mean, I don't know,

00:06:00.238 --> 00:06:02.610
I would say, I'm the one...

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.600
I need Marlon in my life.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:06.860
I never think of money first

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:07.693
and I always need to.

00:06:07.693 --> 00:06:09.560
I always think of dreams first,

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:10.610
I'm such a dreamer.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.230
Our next question comes from Zoey

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.410
who's in Atlanta.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.215
What's your question, Zoey?

00:06:16.215 --> 00:06:18.169
- Hi, Kelly, hi, Cleo.

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:21.887
So I have a blog, a YouTube
channel, and a podcast

00:06:21.887 --> 00:06:23.770
that I've been working really hard on

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.845
this past year, and as
I'm sure both of you know,

00:06:26.845 --> 00:06:28.610
one of the most important things

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.654
about platforms like those
is audience engagement.

00:06:31.654 --> 00:06:35.187
But I have friend who has a blog herself

00:06:35.187 --> 00:06:38.865
and although hers focuses on
slightly different topics,

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:41.650
it seems as though every
time I go to her page,

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:44.531
she has much more audience
engagement than I do.

00:06:44.531 --> 00:06:47.556
And every now and then
I get a little jealous,

00:06:47.556 --> 00:06:49.378
and quite frankly at this age,

00:06:49.378 --> 00:06:52.520
it's kind of hard not to
compare myself to others,

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.130
even to my friends.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.830
So my question for you guys

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.030
is how can I start
embracing and celebrating

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.060
these differences rather than

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.000
making everything a competition?

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.240
- I'm gonna start with Marlon.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:05.850
The only reason why I'm
asking you too, Marlon,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.130
first of all, is you
have a bunch of siblings

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:09.350
and I know I have siblings too,

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.030
and you kind of compete sometimes,

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.960
and compare yourselves,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.190
like am I doing as well as this one.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.040
What do you have as far
as that goes as advice?

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.507
- My mom used to always say,

00:07:19.507 --> 00:07:21.977
"Don't look in somebody else's garden,

00:07:21.977 --> 00:07:23.558
"worry about your grass."

00:07:23.558 --> 00:07:25.828
So I don't wanna be anybody else,

00:07:25.828 --> 00:07:27.027
I wanna be me.

00:07:27.027 --> 00:07:29.010
And I have five siblings

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.709
that are all doing the same thing,

00:07:30.709 --> 00:07:34.438
but I know I'm not them
and they're not me.

00:07:34.438 --> 00:07:36.129
I don't try to compete.

00:07:36.129 --> 00:07:38.150
Think about it, as an artist,

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:39.510
when I go do stand-up,

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.626
sometimes there's 20
other people on the bill,

00:07:42.626 --> 00:07:46.250
am I gonna feel insecure
if I'm watching their set?

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.650
I don't even watch their material,

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:49.180
I don't watch what they do.

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.060
I'm worried about what I'm gonna do

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.080
and what I'm working on

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:54.930
and what energy I'm
gonna bring to the stage.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.531
So you need to focus on you, now.

00:07:58.531 --> 00:08:00.420
If you look at other people's things,

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.120
you need to sit back and not be jealous,

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.860
'cause that's something
that is gonna take you down.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:05.810
Don't feel no type of way.

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:07.140
You could go there to study.

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.190
You can go there to learn.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.750
You can learn, sit there and go,

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.117
wow, that's what's good
about what they're doing.

00:08:12.117 --> 00:08:15.163
But don't try to be them, be you.

00:08:16.691 --> 00:08:17.524
(audience applauds)

00:08:17.524 --> 00:08:18.818
- What about you, Cleo?

00:08:18.818 --> 00:08:23.460
- You know, the person
that you are racing against

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:24.950
in life, is yourself.

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:26.590
And if you think it's anyone else,

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:29.058
than that's just a
delusion, that's not real.

00:08:29.058 --> 00:08:32.330
And so you can distract yourself

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:33.471
away from your journey

00:08:33.471 --> 00:08:36.730
by paying attention to
whatever someone else is doing,

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:38.090
but know that you're actually choosing

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:39.160
to distract yourself,

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:40.730
you're not investing in a reality

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:42.650
that is actually real.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:45.424
Because there is only one you,

00:08:45.424 --> 00:08:49.490
and there's only one unique perspective

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:50.560
that comes from you

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:52.240
and your unique life stories,

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:54.666
and all the things that come with you,

00:08:54.666 --> 00:08:57.640
and so whether they're your closest friend

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.150
in the world or anybody else,

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:01.250
how could that in anyway

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.690
have to do with what you are doing?

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:04.590
And when you look at people,

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:05.640
you know, like Marlon

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:06.750
who are so successful,

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:08.610
it's because, clearly he knows

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.560
how to focus on his own journey.

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:13.467
And that is so incredible

00:09:13.467 --> 00:09:15.184
and it's so important.

00:09:15.184 --> 00:09:18.090
And so I think that one
of the tricks I have,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.818
because I don't think
in a society, as women,

00:09:19.818 --> 00:09:22.638
we are really taught to
kind of compete and compare,

00:09:22.638 --> 00:09:24.970
and we're lucky if we do have the mom

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:26.810
that tells us to tend to our own garden,

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:30.110
but if we don't, we can be
that mom to ourselves too.

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:33.010
And so I would kind of
develop my own mantra

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:33.843
that was like,

00:09:33.843 --> 00:09:35.500
you know what, I'm just used to thinking

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:36.960
I should be competing with my friends,

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.610
when really, when they're doing better,

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:39.460
I'm doing better.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.950
When they shine, I shine.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:43.400
When their light's brighter,

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:44.840
all the light is brighter,

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.100
my light's brighter too,

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:47.787
and the world is a brighter place for it.

00:09:47.787 --> 00:09:49.460
(audience applauds)

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:50.650
- When they're making money,

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.730
girl, you ain't gotta
reach in your pocket.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:53.726
Think about that.

00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:55.228
(Kelly and Cleo laughing)

00:09:55.228 --> 00:09:57.100
- When they're making money,
they're buying dinner.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:58.253
- That's amazing.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.710
Well, thank you so much, Zoey.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:03.030
All right, well we have a question

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:04.525
that came in through Twitter.

00:10:04.525 --> 00:10:07.307
I'm gonna mess this up, my bad.

00:10:07.307 --> 00:10:12.307
@HakunaFatumata wrote,
and I'm really sorry

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:13.903
if I messed that up.

00:10:13.903 --> 00:10:16.891
"My crush only uses
Instagram to message with me

00:10:16.891 --> 00:10:19.567
"he sends heart eyes emojis to me,

00:10:19.567 --> 00:10:21.457
"he hasn't asked for my number.

00:10:21.457 --> 00:10:22.567
"How do I convince him

00:10:22.567 --> 00:10:24.357
"to take our relationship
to the next level

00:10:24.357 --> 00:10:25.320
"and actually text?"

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.140
Oh, I have advice,

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:28.130
I don't know if you want it.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:29.865
Okay, Marlon, why don't you start?

00:10:29.865 --> 00:10:31.200
(audience laughs)

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.081
- First of all, why you yelling at us

00:10:33.081 --> 00:10:34.400
with your text, girl?

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.530
Why you doing all caps?

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:39.038
(everyone laughs)

00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:43.107
Put some lowercase in there. (laughs)

00:10:43.107 --> 00:10:44.860
You know, I'm old fashioned,

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.040
I think a man should court you.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:48.580
And if he's serious,

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.425
then he'll ask you on a date,

00:10:50.425 --> 00:10:52.420
take you on a nice date,

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:53.810
and he'll chase you.

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:56.107
Play hard to get and watch him chase,

00:10:56.107 --> 00:10:58.940
and if he don't chase, let him go.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:00.345
You're the gift, not him.

00:11:00.345 --> 00:11:01.178
(audience applauds)

00:11:01.178 --> 00:11:02.280
- Oh my gosh, I love you.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:03.460
Okay, speak on it.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:04.293
Go ahead, Cleo.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.577
- You know, I think dating is kind of like

00:11:08.577 --> 00:11:11.170
also when you're looking for any job

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:12.330
or anything you want it life,

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.358
you have to be unafraid to lose it.

00:11:14.358 --> 00:11:16.550
Because then if you do have it,

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:17.490
you know it's yours,

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:19.580
and you can also just create boundaries

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:20.440
that help you do that

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.582
if you're not used to being strong enough

00:11:22.582 --> 00:11:25.249
to stay away from your crush.

00:11:25.249 --> 00:11:26.500
If it was one of my girlfriends,

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:27.470
I'd probably be like,

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:29.575
well, take Instagram off
your phone for a week

00:11:29.575 --> 00:11:31.010
and tell him you're doing it,

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:32.100
so that if he wants to
get in touch with you,

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:33.670
he can text you.

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.239
And if he wants to text you,

00:11:35.239 --> 00:11:38.210
or if he actually is
interested in you in that way,

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:39.540
then you'll know by then.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:40.373
And if he doesn't,

00:11:40.373 --> 00:11:42.620
you have to be unafraid to keep it moving.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.106
- Oh, you got game.

00:11:44.106 --> 00:11:45.148
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:11:45.148 --> 00:11:45.981
- [Cleo] I do.

00:11:45.981 --> 00:11:47.239
- I do, I love you.

00:11:47.239 --> 00:11:48.805
And my thing is,

00:11:48.805 --> 00:11:52.860
I don't understand why
you'd wanna be with someone

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:54.757
that only does it in front of people.

00:11:54.757 --> 00:11:55.910
That's my thing.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:57.445
Like, if they're only gonna show you

00:11:57.445 --> 00:11:59.667
they care in front of people.

00:11:59.667 --> 00:12:01.911
You need to know what's
happening behind closed doors,

00:12:01.911 --> 00:12:03.690
not in front of the doors,

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:05.010
'cause that's who you're gonna be with,

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:06.500
just so you know.

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:08.147
And it kind of looks a little like

00:12:08.147 --> 00:12:09.510
"He's Just Not That Into You",

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:10.490
that book, I don't know,

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:11.780
it looks a little like that.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.480
And that's okay 'cause
we've all been there.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.810
Okay, so it's not just you.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.040
But anyway, thank you guys
so much for the advice.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:19.350
You two are such yin and yang,

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.403
and I love this whole panel. (laughs)

